\title{Seed-Compressible Data: Fingerprinting, Routing and Seed Recovery\\
for Generator-Produced Pythagorean Streams}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A pseudorandom or procedural generator turns a short seed into an arbitrarily long file. When such a file appears in a corpus, the optimal encoding of it is its seed, and the compression ratio is unbounded. This paper carries the detection-and-recovery programme through to a complete answer in a setting where every question can be settled exactly: streams of Pythagorean triples produced by the Barning--Berggren generators, three unimodular $3\times3$ integer matrices whose orbits from the root $(3,4,5)$ enumerate every primitive Pythagorean triple exactly once. We establish four results. \emph{Fingerprinting}: by Cayley--Hamilton, every linear readout of every orbit of an integer $3\times3$ matrix $M$ satisfies the order-$3$ linear recurrence with taps $(\det M, -c_2(M), \tr M)$; for the Berggren moves these are $(1,-3,3)$ on the two unipotent branches and $(-1,5,5)$ on the Pell branch. The bound is sharp, and the linear complexity of the hypotenuse stream ($3$ versus $2$) is itself a branch classifier; composite generators driven by a repeated control word remain order-$3$ detectable. \emph{Recovery}: three observed symbols regenerate a fixed-move stream exactly at every index, and for the full ternary generator three sign tests per level recover the control word letter for letter, the code being uniquely decodable. \emph{Coverage versus rarity}: every normalised primitive Pythagorean triple is generator output for exactly one control word, so that corpus is $100\%$ seed-compressible, while the number of length-$n$ files reachable from a seed box $[-N,N]^3$ is at most $3(2N+1)^3$ \emph{independently of $n$}. \emph{Rate dichotomy}: the all-$B$ word of length $k$ emits a hypotenuse $\ge 5\cdot3^k$ (logarithmic seed: genuine compression) while the all-$A$ word emits one $\le 2(k+2)^2$ (square-root seed: catastrophic expansion), and no branch ever beats logarithmic since one step multiplies the hypotenuse by at most $7$. Detectability is governed by the degree of the characteristic polynomial of the driving matrix; profitability by its root moduli.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Compression beyond the pigeonhole bound}

The counting argument that limits lossless compression is airtight: an injective map from $\{0,1\}^n$ into $\bigcup_{m<n}\{0,1\}^m$ cannot exist, so no compressor shortens every input. Practical compression escapes this only because real corpora concentrate on a thin, structured subset of all strings.

Almost all deployed compression exploits \emph{statistical} structure: local correlations captured by a model, coded near the model's entropy. There is a second, qualitatively different kind of structure: some files are \emph{literally generator output}. Procedural game worlds, deterministic simulations, test vectors, tabulated mathematical sequences and certain file-format paddings are the exact orbit of a short deterministic program run from a short seed. For such a file the ideal code length is the seed length, which may be dozens of bits against gigabytes of data.

Exploiting this requires three separable capabilities: a \emph{fingerprint}, a cheap statistic on raw observations indicating generator output without knowledge of the seed; \emph{seed recovery}, an inversion algorithm subject to the falsifiability gate that replaying the recovered seed reproduces the file exactly; and a \emph{router}, a per-file classifier deciding between \emph{seed-compressible} and \emph{model-compressible}, since the seed compressor is useless on almost all inputs.

\subsection{A setting where everything is provable}

We instantiate the programme on the Barning--Berggren generators of Pythagorean triples. The choice is deliberate: it is simultaneously real data (tabulated Pythagorean triples occur in geometry software, test corpora and number-theoretic datasets), genuinely generator-produced with a known finitely presented generator, and small enough that coverage, rarity and compression rate can be determined exactly rather than estimated.

Writing a triple as a column vector $(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}$, the three Barning matrices are
\[
B_A=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\2&-1&2\\2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B_B=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&2\\2&1&2\\2&2&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B_C=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&2\\-2&1&2\\-2&2&3\end{pmatrix},
\]
with associated maps on $\Z^3$
\[
\begin{aligned}
A(a,b,c) &= (a-2b+2c,\; 2a-b+2c,\; 2a-2b+3c),\\
B(a,b,c) &= (a+2b+2c,\; 2a+b+2c,\; 2a+2b+3c),\\
C(a,b,c) &= (-a+2b+2c,\; -2a+b+2c,\; -2a+2b+3c).
\end{aligned}
\]
Each preserves the Lorentz form $a^2+b^2-c^2$, hence maps Pythagorean triples to Pythagorean triples. From the root $(3,4,5)$ they produce $(5,12,13)$, $(21,20,29)$ and $(15,8,17)$ respectively.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{itemize}
\item A universal order-$3$ fingerprint (\S\ref{sec:fingerprint}) valid for any integer $3\times3$ generator matrix, with taps read off the characteristic polynomial, stable under composition with periodic control words.
\item Sharpness of the fingerprint (\S\ref{sec:sharp}), separating unipotent from Pell branches by linear complexity alone.
\item Exact seed recovery (\S\ref{sec:recovery}): three-symbol recovery for fixed-move streams, and a linear-time sign-test decoder for the full ternary code, with unique decodability.
\item A sound, complete and exact router (\S\ref{sec:routing}), together with conserved branch signatures computable from two consecutive observations.
\item Coverage (\S\ref{sec:coverage}): an explicit bijection between control words and normalised primitive triples; and rarity: a length-independent bound on the reach of the seed compressor.
\item The rate dichotomy (\S\ref{sec:rate}): logarithmic seeds on the Pell branch, square-root seeds on the unipotent branches, and a universal logarithmic floor.
\end{itemize}

\section{Preliminaries}

\subsection{Generators, streams, seed compressibility}

\begin{definition}[Generator]
A \emph{generator} on a state space $S$ with output alphabet $O$ is a pair $(\mathrm{next},\mathrm{out})$ with $\mathrm{next}\colon S\to S$ and $\mathrm{out}\colon S\to O$. Its \emph{stream} from seed $s$ is $\sigma_s(t)=\mathrm{out}(\mathrm{next}^{t}(s))$, and its length-$n$ \emph{prefix} is $\sigma_s$ restricted to $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$.
\end{definition}

For the Berggren generators we take $S=O=\Z^3$, $\mathrm{next}$ one of $A,B,C$, and $\mathrm{out}=\mathrm{id}$: the emitted symbol is the whole current triple.

\begin{definition}[Seed compressibility]
A length-$n$ file $x$ is \emph{seed-compressible} for a generator $G$ if $x$ is a length-$n$ prefix of some stream of $G$. If the prefix map is injective the seed is unique, and the \emph{decoder} returns it.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Orbit predicate]
An observed stream $x\colon\N\to\Z^3$ \emph{is an orbit} of a self-map $m$ if $x(t+1)=m(x(t))$ for all $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Orbit $=$ stream from the first symbol]
$x$ is an orbit of $m$ if and only if $x(t)=m^{t}(x(0))$ for all $t$. Hence seed recovery for a triple stream is reading off $x(0)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Forward, induct on $t$ using $x(t+1)=m(x(t))$ and $m^{t+1}=m\circ m^{t}$. Backward, substitute the closed form at $t$ and $t+1$ and use the same iterate identity.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Linear recurrences and linear complexity}

\begin{definition}[LFSR stream]
A sequence $y\colon\N\to\Z$ \emph{satisfies the taps} $(\tau_0,\dots,\tau_{d-1})$ if $y(t+d)=\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}\tau_i y(t+i)$ for all $t\ge0$. Its \emph{linear complexity} is the least such $d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[LFSR generator]
Given taps $\tau$ of order $d$, the associated generator has state the window $(y(t),\dots,y(t+d-1))\in\Z^{d}$, shifts in $\sum_i\tau_i y(t+i)$, and emits the first coordinate. Its seed is the initial window of $d$ symbols.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact reproduction]\label{lem:exact}
If $y$ satisfies taps $\tau$ of order $d$, then the stream of the LFSR generator with taps $\tau$ seeded by $(y(0),\dots,y(d-1))$ equals $y$ at every index.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $t$: after $t$ shifts the state is the window $(y(t),\dots,y(t+d-1))$, since each shifted-in value is forced to equal $y(t+d)$ by the recurrence.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:exact} is the falsifiability gate in pure form: decompressed output is identically, not approximately, the observed data. The Berlekamp--Massey algorithm supplies the practical converse, returning the taps from $2d$ symbols of a sequence of complexity at most $d$.

\subsection{Normalisation}

\begin{definition}
A triple $(a,b,c)$ is \emph{good} if $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $a,b,c>0$; a good triple is a \emph{tree triple} (normalised primitive) if additionally $\gcd(a,b)=1$ and $a$ is odd.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:altc}
For a good triple, $a<c$ and $b<c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $a^2+b^2=c^2$ with all entries positive, $a^2<c^2$; positivity gives $a<c$, and symmetrically $b<c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Invariance of normalisation]\label{lem:norm}
Each of $A,B,C$ maps good triples to good triples and tree triples to tree triples.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Preservation of $a^2+b^2-c^2$ is a direct computation. Positivity of the images follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:altc} by linear arithmetic (e.g.\ $a-2b+2c>0$ because $c>b$). Primitivity persists because any common divisor of the image legs divides the preimage legs, the matrices being unimodular; and the first coordinate changes by an even amount, so its parity is preserved.
\end{proof}

\section{Fingerprinting}\label{sec:fingerprint}

\subsection{The general theorem}

For $M\in\Z^{3\times3}$ let $\tr M$ be its trace, $\det M$ its determinant, and
\[
c_2(M)=(M_{00}M_{11}-M_{01}M_{10})+(M_{00}M_{22}-M_{02}M_{20})+(M_{11}M_{22}-M_{12}M_{21})
\]
the sum of the principal $2\times2$ minors, so that the characteristic polynomial is $\lambda^3-(\tr M)\lambda^2+c_2(M)\lambda-\det M$.

\begin{theorem}[Cayley--Hamilton in readout form]\label{thm:ch}
For every $M\in\Z^{3\times3}$ and all $u,w\in\Z^3$,
\[
u\cdot M^3w=\tr(M)\bigl(u\cdot M^2w\bigr)-c_2(M)\bigl(u\cdot Mw\bigr)+\det(M)(u\cdot w).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
This is the relation $M^3=(\tr M)M^2-c_2(M)M+(\det M)I$ paired against $u$ and $w$; expanding both sides as polynomials in the entries verifies it directly.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal fingerprint]\label{thm:univ}
For every $M\in\Z^{3\times3}$, every observation functional $u$ and every seed $v$, the readout $y(t)=u\cdot M^{t}v$ satisfies the order-$3$ taps $(\det M,\,-c_2(M),\,\tr M)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ch} with $w=M^{t}v$ and rewrite $M^{t+j}v=M^{j}(M^{t}v)$.
\end{proof}

The taps depend on neither the seed nor the observation functional: a detector need not know what it is measuring. The linear complexity of \emph{any} observable is at most $3$, so a complexity probe on six samples of any coordinate returns the generator's characteristic data.

\subsection{The Berggren taps and closed forms}

\begin{corollary}[Branch fingerprints]\label{cor:taps}
For all $u,v,w\in\Z$ and every seed $p$, the readout $t\mapsto u\,a_t+v\,b_t+w\,c_t$ satisfies the taps $(1,-3,3)$ along the $A$- and $C$-orbits and the taps $(-1,5,5)$ along the $B$-orbit.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\tr B_A=3$, $c_2(B_A)=3$, $\det B_A=1$, giving characteristic polynomial $(\lambda-1)^3$; identically for $B_C$. For $B_B$, $\tr=5$, $c_2=-5$, $\det=-1$, giving $\lambda^3-5\lambda^2-5\lambda+1=(\lambda+1)(\lambda^2-6\lambda+1)$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:univ}.
\end{proof}

The taps $(1,-3,3)$ say exactly that the third difference vanishes, i.e.\ the stream is a quadratic polynomial in $t$.

\begin{theorem}[Closed forms on the unipotent branches]\label{thm:closed}
For every $(a,b,c)$ and $t\ge0$,
\begin{align*}
A^{t}(a,b,c) &= \bigl(a+2t(c-b),\; b+2t(a-b+c)+2t(t-1)(c-b),\; c+2t(a-b+c)+2t(t-1)(c-b)\bigr),\\
C^{t}(a,b,c) &= \bigl(a+2t(-a+b+c)+2t(t-1)(c-a),\; b+2t(c-a),\; c+2t(-a+b+c)+2t(t-1)(c-a)\bigr).
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $t$; the successor step is a polynomial identity in $a,b,c,t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Benchmark families]\label{cor:bench}
From the root,
\[
A^{t}(3,4,5)=(2t+3,\;2t^2+6t+4,\;2t^2+6t+5),\qquad C^{t}(3,4,5)=(4t^2+8t+3,\;4t+4,\;4t^2+8t+5).
\]
In particular the $A$-branch first leg and the $C$-branch second leg are arithmetic progressions with common differences $2(c-b)$ and $2(c-a)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The Pell factor]\label{thm:pell}
Along any $B$-orbit, $c_{t+2}=6c_{t+1}-c_t$ and $(a+b)_{t+2}=6(a+b)_{t+1}-(a+b)_t$, while the leg difference alternates: $b_t-a_t=(-1)^t(b_0-a_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
On the $B$-branch the pair $(a+b,c)$ evolves by $\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}3&4\\2&3\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, with characteristic polynomial $\lambda^2-6\lambda+1$; the remaining eigendirection, spanned by $b-a$, has eigenvalue $-1$. Substituting two and three successive $B$-steps and simplifying gives each identity.
\end{proof}

From the root the $B$-hypotenuses are $5,29,169,985,5741,\dots$, with ratio tending to $3+2\sqrt2\approx5.828$.

\subsection{Sharpness and complexity as a classifier}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
(i) The $A$-branch hypotenuse stream from the root, $c_t=2t^2+6t+5$, satisfies no order-$2$ recurrence; likewise the $C$-branch stream $c_t=4t^2+8t+5$. (ii) The $B$-branch hypotenuse stream satisfies no order-$1$ recurrence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(i) The instances $t=0,1,2$ give $25=5\gamma_0+13\gamma_1$, $41=13\gamma_0+25\gamma_1$, $61=25\gamma_0+41\gamma_1$, an inconsistent system. (ii) $c_0=5$, $c_1=29$, and $29=5\gamma_0$ has no integer solution.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Linear complexity separates the branches]\label{cor:sep}
The hypotenuse stream from the root has linear complexity exactly $3$ on the $A$- and $C$-branches and exactly $2$ on the $B$-branch. Measuring the complexity of a single observed coordinate therefore distinguishes the exponential branch from the unipotent ones, with no seed search.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Composite generators}

Let $w\in\{A,B,C\}^*$ and let $M_w$ be the product of the corresponding Barning matrices in application order.

\begin{theorem}[Periodic control words]\label{thm:word}
For any control word $w$, functional $u$ and seed $p$, the stream $t\mapsto u\cdot(\text{apply }w)^{t}(p)$ satisfies the order-$3$ taps $(\det M_w,\,-c_2(M_w),\,\tr M_w)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Applying $w$ once is multiplication by $M_w$ (induction on $w$), so the composite orbit is the orbit of a single integer $3\times3$ matrix and Theorem~\ref{thm:univ} applies.
\end{proof}

\section{Seed recovery}\label{sec:recovery}

\subsection{Fixed-move streams}

\begin{theorem}[Three-symbol recovery]\label{thm:three}
Let $y(t)=u\,a_t+v\,b_t+w\,c_t$ be any linear readout of an $A$-orbit. The LFSR generator with taps $(1,-3,3)$ seeded by $(y(0),y(1),y(2))$ reproduces $y(t)$ for every $t$. Likewise for the $C$-branch with taps $(1,-3,3)$ and the $B$-branch with taps $(-1,5,5)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:taps} with Lemma~\ref{lem:exact}.
\end{proof}

An observed file of $n$ triples on a fixed branch therefore encodes into a two-bit branch label plus three integers, with compression ratio unbounded in $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Seed uniqueness]
Since the first emitted symbol is the seed, the length-$n$ prefix map of a Berggren generator is injective for every $n\ge1$; any decoder returning \emph{a} consistent seed returns \emph{the} seed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Backward recovery]
Each move is invertible over $\Z$ ($\det=\pm1$), and applying the inverse $t$ times to the $t$-th orbit point returns the seed exactly.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The path code}

For the full generator the seed is a control word $w\in\{A,B,C\}^*$ and the output is $\path(w)$, the result of applying $w$ to the root.

\begin{lemma}[Growth]\label{lem:growth}
For every good triple $p$ and step $s$, $c(s\,p)\ge c(p)+4$; consequently $c(\path(w))\ge5+4|w|$, i.e.\ $|w|\le(c-5)/4$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The increments are $c(Ap)-c(p)=2(a+c-b)$, $c(Bp)-c(p)=2(a+b+c)$ and $c(Cp)-c(p)=2(b+c-a)$, each twice a sum of two positive integers by Lemma~\ref{lem:altc}, hence at least $4$. Induct along the word from $c(\path(\varepsilon))=5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Parent uniqueness]\label{lem:parent}
If $p,p'$ have positive legs and $s\,p=s'\,p'$, then $s=s'$ and $p=p'$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each pair of distinct moves is separated by a sign obstruction: e.g.\ $Ap=Bp'$ forces $a-2b+2c=a'+2b'+2c'$ and $2a-b+2c=2a'+b'+2c'$ simultaneously, contradicting $b,b'>0$ by linear arithmetic. Once $s=s'$, injectivity of the unimodular move gives $p=p'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique decodability]\label{thm:inj}
$\path$ is injective: distinct control words emit distinct triples.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on word length. If exactly one word is empty, Lemma~\ref{lem:growth} forces different hypotenuses ($5$ versus at least $9$). Otherwise write $w=u\cdot s$, $w'=u'\cdot s'$; then $s\,\path(u)=s'\,\path(u')$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:parent} gives $s=s'$ and $\path(u)=\path(u')$, and the induction hypothesis gives $u=u'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Sign-test decoder]
For an observed triple $q$ define $\mathrm{parentStep}(q)$ by trying the inverse moves in order: return $A$ if $A^{-1}q$ has both legs positive, else $B$ if $B^{-1}q$ does, else $C$ if $C^{-1}q$ does, else nothing; let $\mathrm{parentOf}(q)$ be the corresponding preimage. Then
\[
\mathrm{decode}(0,q)=\varepsilon,\qquad
\mathrm{decode}(n+1,q)=
\begin{cases}
\varepsilon & \text{if }\mathrm{parentStep}(q)\text{ is undefined},\\
\mathrm{decode}(n,\mathrm{parentOf}(q))\cdot s & \text{if }\mathrm{parentStep}(q)=s.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[One-symbol recovery]\label{thm:one}
If $p$ has positive legs then $\mathrm{parentStep}(s\,p)=s$ and $\mathrm{parentOf}(s\,p)=p$ for every step $s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Three case analyses. For $s=A$ the $A$-test fires because $A^{-1}(Ap)=p$ has positive legs. For $s=B$ the $A$-test provably fails on $Bp$ (a linear-arithmetic contradiction with $a,b>0$) and the $B$-test then fires; for $s=C$ both earlier tests fail.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact seed recovery: the falsifiability gate]\label{thm:decode}
For every control word $w$, $\mathrm{decode}(|w|,\path(w))=w$; replaying the recovered word reproduces the observed triple exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $w$ from the right, using Theorem~\ref{thm:one} at each peel.
\end{proof}

With Lemma~\ref{lem:growth}, the decoder halts after at most $(c-5)/4$ rounds of three sign tests: recovery is linear in the seed length and uses only integer additions and comparisons.

\section{Routing}\label{sec:routing}

\subsection{Conserved branch signatures}

\begin{theorem}[Invariants]\label{thm:inv}
For all $t$: $c_t-b_t=c_0-b_0$ on the $A$-branch; $c_t-a_t=c_0-a_0$ on the $C$-branch; and $|b_t-a_t|=|b_0-a_0|$ on the $B$-branch.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first two follow by subtracting coordinates in Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}, where the quadratic parts cancel identically; the third follows from $b_t-a_t=(-1)^t(b_0-a_0)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:pell}).
\end{proof}

These are two-subtraction tests on consecutive observations, cheaper even than fitting a recurrence.

\subsection{A sound, complete, exact classifier}

\begin{theorem}[Separation]\label{thm:sep}
If $p$ has positive legs then $Ap$, $Bp$, $Cp$ are pairwise distinct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$Ap=Bp$ forces $b=0$; $Ap=Cp$ forces $a=0$; $Bp=Cp$ forces $a=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
Given consecutive observations $p,q$, let $\which(p,q)$ be $A$ if $Ap=q$, else $B$ if $Bp=q$, else $C$ if $Cp=q$, else undefined.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:classifier}
The classifier is \emph{sound} (if $\which(p,q)=s$ then $s\,p=q$), \emph{complete} (if $s\,p=q$ for some $s$, then $\which(p,q)$ is defined) and \emph{exact on nondegenerate data} (if $p$ has positive legs and $s\,p=q$, then $\which(p,q)=s$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Soundness and completeness are immediate by case analysis on which test fires. Exactness uses Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}: the three candidate images are distinct, so at most one test can fire, and by completeness exactly one does.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unambiguous routing]
If an observed stream with positive legs at $t=0$ is an orbit of both $s$ and $s'$ then $s=s'$: seed and two-bit family label are both uniquely recoverable.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Negative benchmark items}

\begin{theorem}[Constant streams are not orbits]\label{thm:const}
Let $(a,b,c)$ be a Pythagorean triple with all entries positive. The constant stream $x(t)=(a,b,c)$ is not an orbit of $A$, $B$ or $C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity gives $a<c$ and $b<c$; each move then strictly increases the hypotenuse (Lemma~\ref{lem:growth}), contradicting $x(1)=x(0)$.
\end{proof}

Constant data is maximally \emph{model}-compressible and provably not seed-compressible: the two regimes are genuinely different, and the router must distinguish them.

\begin{theorem}[Rescaling destroys seed compressibility]\label{thm:rescale}
For every $p$ with positive legs, $Ap$, $Bp$ and $Cp$ all differ from $(6,8,10)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $Ap=(6,8,10)$ then $a-2b+2c=6$ and $2a-b+2c=8$ give $a+b=2$, hence $a=b=1$ and $2c=7$, impossible. If $Bp=(6,8,10)$ then $a-b=2$ and $3b+2c=4$ with $b,c>0$, impossible. The case of $C$ is symmetric to the first.
\end{proof}

Thus $(6,8,10)$, the doubling of the root, has no admissible parent and lies outside the range of the control-word code altogether; scaling a covered file by an integer factor removes it from the path code and forces the compressor back to spelling out a state, so normalisation must precede detection. (The rescaled file remains an orbit of the same linear move, so the fixed-move compressor still applies, with a seed of three full-size integers rather than a short control word.)

\section{Coverage and rarity}\label{sec:coverage}

\begin{theorem}[Descent]\label{thm:descent}
Every tree triple $p$ with hypotenuse $c>5$ has a step $s$ and a tree triple $q$ with $c(q)<c(p)$ and $s\,q=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exactly one inverse move lands in the positive cone (a case analysis on the signs of $a-b$ and $a+b-c$). That preimage is Pythagorean since the moves preserve the Lorentz form and are invertible; it is primitive because any common divisor of its legs divides its hypotenuse and hence, by unimodularity, both legs of $p$; and the parent hypotenuse $3c-2a-2b$ is strictly less than $c$ because $a+b>c$ for positive Pythagorean triples.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Base case]\label{thm:base}
The only tree triple with hypotenuse at most $5$ is $(3,4,5)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity and Lemma~\ref{lem:altc} bound $a,b\le4$; a finite check against $a^2+b^2=c^2$, $\gcd(a,b)=1$ and $a$ odd leaves only $(3,4,5)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coverage]\label{thm:cover}
Every tree triple is $\path(w)$ for some control word $w$: the corpus of normalised primitive Pythagorean triples is $100\%$ seed-compressible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on the hypotenuse. If $c\le5$ then $p=(3,4,5)=\path(\varepsilon)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:base}. Otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:descent} gives a tree-triple parent $q$ of strictly smaller positive hypotenuse; by induction $q=\path(u)$, whence $p=\path(u\cdot s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The path code is a bijection]\label{thm:bij}
A triple $p$ is a tree triple if and only if there is exactly one control word $w$ with $\path(w)=p$. Hence $\path$ is a bijection from $\{A,B,C\}^*$ onto the set of normalised primitive Pythagorean triples.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence is Theorem~\ref{thm:cover}, uniqueness Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}; conversely every emitted triple is a tree triple by Lemma~\ref{lem:norm} and induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every tree triple $p$ there is a control word $w$ with $\mathrm{decode}(|w|,p)=w$, $\path(w)=p$ and $|w|\le(c-5)/4$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The reach over arbitrary files}

Fix $N\ge1$ and let the \emph{seed box} be $\{-N,\dots,N\}^3$, of cardinality $(2N+1)^3$. Let $W_N(n)$ be the set of length-$n$ files obtainable as a prefix of an orbit of $A$, $B$ or $C$ from a seed in the box.

\begin{theorem}[Length-independent capacity]\label{thm:capacity}
$|W_N(n)|\le 3(2N+1)^3$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$W_N(n)$ is a union of three images of the seed box under prefix maps; an image has at most the cardinality of its source, and a union of three sets at most the sum of their cardinalities.
\end{proof}

What is absent from the bound is $n$: adding symbols gives the compressor no extra reach, since the seed determines the entire infinite orbit.

\begin{theorem}[Most files are not seed-compressible]\label{thm:rarity}
For $N\ge1$ and $n\ge2$ there is a length-$n$ file with all symbols in the seed box lying outside $W_N(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
There are $\bigl((2N+1)^3\bigr)^{n}$ candidate files. With $K=2N+1\ge3$ we have $K^3\ge27$, hence $(K^3)^n\ge(K^3)^2=K^3\cdot K^3\ge27K^3>3K^3\ge|W_N(n)|$, so containment is impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
 & natural corpus (primitive triples) & arbitrary files over the same alphabet\\
\midrule
fraction seed-compressible & $1$ & $\le 3(2N+1)^{3}/(2N+1)^{3n}\to0$\\
seed size & $|w|\le(c-5)/4$ symbols & ---\\
recovery cost & $3|w|$ sign tests & ---\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Coverage and rarity are complementary, not contradictory.}
\end{table}

A seed compressor is not a general-purpose codec but a detector with an enormous payoff on the thin set it recognises. Hence the architecture: cheap fingerprint first, full recovery only when it fires, statistical model otherwise.

\section{The rate dichotomy}\label{sec:rate}

\begin{theorem}[Unipotent branch: square-root seeds]\label{thm:Arate}
The all-$A$ word of length $k$ emits the hypotenuse $c=2k^2+6k+5\le2(k+2)^2$. Hence the seed length is $k\approx\sqrt{c/2}$, whereas writing $c$ in binary costs $\Theta(\log c)$ bits: seed coding is exponentially worse than the raw encoding.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:bench} at $t=k$ and note $2k^2+6k+5\le2k^2+8k+8$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pell branch: logarithmic seeds]\label{thm:Brate}
The all-$B$ word of length $k$ emits a hypotenuse $c\ge5\cdot3^{k}$; hence $k\le\log_3(c/5)$, which is genuine compression.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $B$-step at least triples the hypotenuse, $c\mapsto2a+2b+3c\ge3c$ for positive legs; induct from $c=5$.
\end{proof}

The true growth rate is $3+2\sqrt2\approx5.828$, the dominant root of $\lambda^2-6\lambda+1$; the factor $3$ is what a clean induction gives.

\begin{theorem}[Universal logarithmic floor]\label{thm:floor}
One step multiplies the hypotenuse by at most $7$; hence $c(\path(w))\le5\cdot7^{|w|}$, i.e.\ $|w|\ge\log_7(c/5)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $a,b<c$, each image hypotenuse $\pm2a\pm2b+3c$ is at most $7c$; induct along the word.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rate dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
For every $k$,
\[
c\bigl(\path(B^k)\bigr)\ \ge\ 5\cdot3^{k},\qquad c\bigl(\path(A^k)\bigr)\ \le\ 2(k+2)^2 .
\]
At $k=10$ the all-$B$ seed of ten ternary symbols ($\approx16$ bits) names a hypotenuse of at least $295{,}245$, while ten all-$A$ symbols name only $265$.
\end{theorem}

Both branches are equally detectable: the same order-$3$ fingerprint, the same three-symbol recovery, the same detector cost. They differ entirely in profit, and the separating quantity is the spectral radius: $\rho(B_B)=3+2\sqrt2>1$ against $\rho(B_A)=\rho(B_C)=1$.

\begin{remark}[Principle]
Detectability is governed by the degree of the characteristic polynomial of the driving matrix; profitability by its root moduli. A generator can be certainly detected, its seed exactly recovered, and the resulting encoding still be larger than the data.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n$ be the file length in triples and $k$ the recovered seed length.

\paragraph{A1. Fingerprint probe.} Run Berlekamp--Massey on an observed integer stream to obtain the minimal taps and complexity $L$. Cost $O(n^2)$ integer operations, or $O(n\log^2n)$ with fast variants. Here $L\le3$ always; $L=2$ with taps $(-1,6)$ indicates the Pell branch, $L=3$ with taps $(1,-3,3)$ a unipotent branch.

\paragraph{A2. Invariant probe.} Compute $c_t-b_t$, $c_t-a_t$, $|b_t-a_t|$ across the file; a branch is admissible if the corresponding quantity is constant. Cost $O(n)$ subtractions; rejects most non-generator data instantly.

\paragraph{A3. Transition classifier.} Apply $A,B,C$ to $x(t)$ and compare with $x(t+1)$: $O(1)$ per transition, sound, complete and exact on positive-leg data (Theorem~\ref{thm:classifier}).

\paragraph{A4. Path decoder.} Peel one control symbol per round by three sign tests until reaching $(3,4,5)$: $3k$ tests with $k\le(c-5)/4$, total $O(k\log c)$ bit operations; correctness is Theorems~\ref{thm:one} and~\ref{thm:decode}.

The complete compressor is A2 (reject fast) $\to$ A1 (confirm and identify family) $\to$ A3/A4 (recover the seed) $\to$ verify by replay $\to$ fall back to a model-based codec if the replay is not bit-identical or if the seed is longer than the data. Verification by replay is what makes the scheme safe: seed compression is attempted, never trusted.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Corpus triage.} Tabulated Pythagorean triples, procedurally generated right-triangle meshes and integer-geometry test suites are fully covered by Theorem~\ref{thm:cover}, so the ideal encoding is the control word, recoverable in linear time; the invariant probe makes the test essentially free on inputs that are not covered.

\paragraph{Generator identification in the abstract.} Theorems~\ref{thm:univ} and~\ref{thm:word} concern any integer $3\times3$ generator. A linear state machine over $\Z^{d}$ cannot hide from a linear-complexity probe on any linear observable, nor by being run in periodic blocks, since a product of $d\times d$ matrices is again $d\times d$. What defeats the probe is nonlinearity in the update or the output function --- the design principle of cryptographically strong generators, seen from the attacker's side.

\paragraph{A caution about ``compressible''.} Theorem~\ref{thm:const} exhibits data that is trivially model-compressible and provably not seed-compressible; Theorem~\ref{thm:Arate} exhibits data that is exactly seed-recoverable and yet seed-\emph{in}compressible. Both failure modes are invisible to a detector that only measures linear complexity.

\paragraph{Normalisation matters.} Theorem~\ref{thm:rescale} shows that multiplying a covered file by $2$ moves it outside the reach of the generator; a deployment needs an explicit normalisation stage whose overhead a coverage theorem alone conceals.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The family is narrow: three fixed unimodular matrices in dimension three. The fingerprint theorem generalises verbatim to any dimension, but coverage and rate use specific arithmetic of the Barning matrices. The rarity bound is stated for a bounded seed box. Finally we treat exact integer data; noisy observations would require a robust complexity probe.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Spectral compression law]
For a generator driven by an integer matrix $M\in\mathrm{GL}_3(\Z)$ with spectral radius $\rho(M)>1$, the control-word length needed to reach a state of size $S$ is $\Theta(\log S/\log\rho(M))$; if $\rho(M)=1$ (unipotent) it is $\Theta(S^{1/d})$, where $d$ is the size of the largest Jordan block minus one.
\end{conjecture}

Detectability and compressibility are governed by two different invariants of the same matrix: detectability by the degree of the characteristic polynomial (always $3$ here, by Cayley--Hamilton), compressibility by its root moduli. Theorem~\ref{thm:univ} gives the degree half in full generality and Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} the two extreme cases of the root half; only the interpolation is missing.

\begin{conjecture}[A $\Theta(\sqrt c)$ lower bound for unipotent seeds is unavoidable]
For every control word $w$, $c(\path(w))\ge2|w|^2+6|w|+5$, with equality exactly for the all-$A$ word.
\end{conjecture}

That is, the all-$A$ path is the slowest path in the whole tree, so the worst-case seed length is exactly $\Theta(\sqrt c)$. The hypotenuse increment $c'-c=2(a+c-b)$ is minimised by keeping the legs as unbalanced as possible, which is precisely what the unipotent branch does. Lemma~\ref{lem:growth} gives the linear bound $4|w|\le c-5$ by a one-line induction and Corollary~\ref{cor:bench} the conjectured extremal value; the missing ingredient is a monotone potential function on the tree.

\begin{conjecture}[Berlekamp--Massey as a universal router]
For a stream produced by any fixed integer $d\times d$ generator matrix, the linear complexity returned on $2d$ samples of a generic linear observable equals the degree of the minimal polynomial of the matrix, and the resulting taps together with $d$ samples determine the entire stream. A single complexity probe then suffices both to route the file and to name the generator family, with no seed search.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions: extend coverage to non-primitive data via a normalisation front end with provable overhead; quantify the reach for unbounded seeds using a description-length prior instead of a box; study robustness of the complexity probe under perturbation; and identify the minimal nonlinearity defeating every linear-complexity probe while keeping the update efficiently invertible.

\section{Conclusion}

Generator output leaves an unavoidable order-$3$ linear signature on every linear observable (Theorem~\ref{thm:univ}), stable under periodic control words (Theorem~\ref{thm:word}) and sharp enough that the complexity value alone identifies the branch (Corollary~\ref{cor:sep}). Recovery is exact: three symbols for a fixed-move stream (Theorem~\ref{thm:three}), a linear-time sign-test descent for the full ternary code (Theorem~\ref{thm:decode}), with unique decodability (Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}). Routing is sound, complete and exact (Theorem~\ref{thm:classifier}), with explicit rejected benchmark items (Theorems~\ref{thm:const} and~\ref{thm:rescale}). Coverage on the natural corpus is total (Theorem~\ref{thm:bij}) while the reach over arbitrary files does not grow with the file length (Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}).

The most transferable finding is the rate dichotomy (Theorem~\ref{thm:dich}): two branches of the same generator, with identical detectability, one compressing logarithmically and the other expanding by an exponential factor, separated only by whether the driving matrix has spectral radius greater than $1$. Finding the seed is the easy half; profiting from it is what decides whether a compressor beats the pigeonhole bound on a given file.

